---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getStats } from '../lib/api';
const s = await getStats();
const localMode = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_LOCAL_MODE === 'true';
---
<Base title="About — FOMO Robinhood Radar" section="about"
      description="How FOMO Robinhood Radar resolves a fomo.family profile to a real Robinhood Chain wallet, tracks it for free, and judges whether the result is skill or one lucky bag.">
  <div class="pagehead"><h1>About</h1></div>
  <p class="subhead">what this is and how it works</p>

  {localMode ? <section style="margin-bottom:30px">
    <h3>Your local research pipeline</h3>
    <p>This installation discovers wallets through the public RH Trenches directory and collects
      their fills from the Robinhood Chain RPC. It keeps its own database and works backward
      toward seven days of history. The watcher's target interval is 20 seconds; requests and
      rate limits can make a pass take longer.</p>
    <h3>Automatic scores, with visible evidence</h3>
    <p>The local scorer uses transparent rules over verified, priced fills. It matches position
      cycles and excludes unknown inventory, gifts, and unverified fills. It does not use the
      author's wallet scores or train a neural copy of a trader. A wallet's page shows the
      evidence and limitations behind its score.</p>
    <p>Default signal eligibility needs 20 usable verified fills, eight closed cycles across four
      tokens, and 24 hours of observed activity. Large unresolved open cost or one dominant win
      keeps a wallet below the signal cutoff. The 30 bps cost buffer on each leg is an assumption;
      it does not reproduce the fees or prices a follower would actually receive.</p>
    <h3>What a signal means</h3>
    <p>The signals feed requires at least two buyers scoring 60 or higher. Bursts use first buys
      inside a rolling window and sum each wallet's squared score divided by 100 squared.
      The Bursts page shows its configured threshold. A score ranks an observed sample and
      does not predict future profit.</p>
    <h3>Operating this version</h3>
    <p>The homepage checks watcher, collector, and scorer freshness separately. An empty feed
      can mean that the system is still collecting enough evidence. Collection and scoring
      run automatically while this Mac is signed in, awake, and online. This installation
      has no brokerage connection or funded order execution.</p>
    <p>Open <a href="/leaderboard?status=all">every wallet</a> to inspect scores or
      <a href="http://127.0.0.1:8767/docs">the local API documentation</a> to inspect the data.</p>
  </section> : <div>
  <p style="font-size:16px;margin-bottom:25px">
    The fomo.family leaderboard shows a number. It does not show whether that number came from
    skill or one lucky ticket, which wallet the person actually trades from, what they are
    buying this minute, or whether they even bought it. This fills in all four, on nothing but
    public endpoints, and reads the chain every twenty seconds.
  </p>

  <h3 style="margin:0 0 12px">Four things that took a while to learn</h3>
  <ol class="roster" style="margin-bottom:25px">
    <li>
      <div class="score follow" style="font-size:22px">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="top"><span class="handle">A fomo profile address is not a wallet</span></div>
        <p>Every address the fomo API returns is an internal account with zero on-chain history.
        The real one is inferred by intersecting who traded the same token in the same minute across
        a dozen of a trader's swaps, weighting quiet windows higher. Graded against fomo's own
        verified wallets once those were available: 101 agreements, none the other way.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="score follow" style="font-size:22px">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="top"><span class="handle">That PnL is real money, and it includes open bags</span></div>
        <p>A wallet showing millions on a few thousand of on-chain outlay is not a glitch: the
        position ran and was never sold. Negative on-chain cash flow is what accumulating looks
        like, not what losing looks like.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="score follow" style="font-size:22px">03</div>
      <div>
        <div class="top"><span class="handle">A relayer submits every trade</span></div>
        <p>The trader's wallet is never the transaction's sender, so searching by sender finds
        nothing. Each fill routes through one contract, and the wallet's leg facing it gives the
        direction. That is why tracking the whole roster costs two requests and no money.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="score follow" style="font-size:22px">04</div>
      <div>
        <div class="top"><span class="handle">A token arriving from the router is not always the wallet buying it</span></div>
        <p>Anyone can call the router with their own ETH and name a famous wallet as the recipient,
        and anyone can push fifty cents of a token into eighteen such wallets through fomo's own
        flow. To a tracker that reads "arrived from a swap" as "bought", both are the cohort piling
        in. Every fill here is checked against its receipt: a swap sent to the router itself is
        nobody's trade, a buy far below the wallet's own size is dust, and a token pushed into more
        trusted wallets than bought it for real is quarantined from every feed. About one buy in
        eleven credited to a trusted wallet turns out not to be that wallet's.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3 style="margin:0 0 12px">Reading the numbers</h3>
  <div class="cols" style="margin-bottom:25px">
    <p class="note"><b>Conviction</b> is the sum of each holder's (score/100)². It says whose money
    is in a name rather than how many wallets are, because anyone can open a wallet.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>Mult</b> is what a position is worth against what it cost. A dash means fomo
    reports profit already withdrawn, so the entry price cannot be recovered.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>USDG and WETH are quote assets</b>, never positions. Feeds built on raw DEX
    data rank the stablecoin every trade passes through as the most-bought token on the chain.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>A burst</b> is conviction with a clock on it: four wallets scoring 80, say,
    all making their first buy of a token inside half an hour. Caught within twenty seconds of the
    fill that tips it. Replayed over the whole tape, it fires about five times a day and 39% reach
    2× within a day — better on every measure than the plain feed, and still a coin with a long tail.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>dust</b>, <b>gift</b> and <b>seeded</b> on a fill or a token mean it was
    pushed at the wallet rather than bought by it. Such fills are shown and never counted.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>A score judges process, not price.</b> It is an opinion about whether a
    result looks repeatable. It is not a prediction.</p>
  </div>
  </div>}

  {s && <dl class="strip">
    <div><dt>Traders</dt><dd>{s.scored}</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Fills</dt><dd>{s.fills.toLocaleString('en-US')}</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Positions</dt><dd>{s.positions}</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Tokens named</dt><dd>{s.tokens.toLocaleString('en-US')}</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Cost / month</dt><dd class="g">$0</dd></div>
  </dl>}

  <p class="note">
    <b>This is research, not advice.</b> Every number here describes what other wallets have
    already done. Nothing on this site is a recommendation to buy or sell anything, and a wallet
    that was right about its last twenty positions can be wrong about the next one.
  </p>
</Base>
